WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.920
Okay, so what is performance analysis?

00:05.960 --> 00:12.760
It is the process of comparing the current value with a target value to compare the performance of specific

00:12.760 --> 00:13.280
category.

00:13.280 --> 00:17.160
And this can help us in order to measure the success to compare the performance.

00:17.160 --> 00:18.920
So the formula for that is very simple.

00:18.920 --> 00:24.280
We're going to find the difference between the current measure and the target measure by subtracting

00:24.280 --> 00:24.440
them.

00:24.480 --> 00:29.600
Like for example, we can go and compare the current sale with the average sale or the current year

00:29.640 --> 00:35.560
sales with the previous year sales, or the current sales with the lowest sales, or maybe the highest

00:35.560 --> 00:36.000
sales.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.800
So as you can see, we are always comparing the current measure together with a target with something

00:40.800 --> 00:41.080
else.

00:41.080 --> 00:44.760
So for example, we have here again a measure that is splitted by three categories.

00:44.760 --> 00:47.000
So those values are the current values.

00:47.040 --> 00:49.480
Now if you have a target like for example the average.

00:49.520 --> 00:52.000
Now as you can see for each row we have like the 200.

00:52.040 --> 00:56.840
Now what we can do once we have those two things in one row, we can go and simply subtract them.

00:56.840 --> 01:00.480
So for the A the current value is exactly equal to the average.

01:00.520 --> 01:04.000
Both of them is 200 and the difference between them is zero.

01:04.000 --> 01:06.560
So this product is performing as an average.

01:06.600 --> 01:09.560
Now for the next one we have 300 and the target is 200.

01:09.560 --> 01:11.720
So the differences between them is 100.

01:11.760 --> 01:14.880
That means this category is performing very well.

01:14.880 --> 01:16.480
So this is a good performer.

01:16.520 --> 01:19.040
Now for the last one we will get -100.

01:19.080 --> 01:21.320
So that means it is below the average.

01:21.320 --> 01:23.120
So it is not performing very well.

01:23.120 --> 01:28.080
And for this type of analysis we usually use window functions like the aggregate window functions,

01:28.120 --> 01:32.920
the sum average, max min or the value in the functions like lead and lag.

01:32.960 --> 01:38.360
So now let's go back to SQL and apply this formula in order to measure the performance of our business.

01:38.360 --> 01:39.000
So let's go.

01:39.040 --> 01:39.840
All right my friends.

01:39.840 --> 01:41.280
So now we have the following task.

01:41.320 --> 01:47.960
Analyze the yearly performance of products by comparing their sales to both the average sales performance

01:47.960 --> 01:50.960
of the products and the previous year's sales.

01:51.000 --> 01:53.640
Okay, this sounds a little bit complicated and serious.

01:53.640 --> 01:55.440
Let's have some coffee before we start.

01:56.640 --> 01:58.960
Okay, so what do we have over here?

01:58.960 --> 02:02.680
So it is talking about the yearly performance of products.

02:02.680 --> 02:08.520
So that means we need the order date as a dimension, and as well the products and the measure that

02:08.520 --> 02:10.560
is used over here is the sales.

02:10.560 --> 02:12.000
So let's do it step by step.

02:12.000 --> 02:15.080
So we need things from our fact table.

02:15.080 --> 02:17.840
So fact sales and we need the product.

02:17.840 --> 02:22.480
So I'm going to go and get it from the dimension products in order to have a nice name.

02:23.560 --> 02:26.640
So we have to join the data by the product key.

02:27.120 --> 02:29.760
And I'm going to go and change the alias to P.

02:30.120 --> 02:31.960
So product key okay.

02:32.000 --> 02:33.760
So with that we have our two tables.

02:33.800 --> 02:35.560
Now let's go and select our columns.

02:35.560 --> 02:37.800
So we need the order dates.

02:37.800 --> 02:41.400
We need the product name and we need our measure.

02:41.400 --> 02:42.960
So it's going to be the sales amount.

02:42.960 --> 02:43.400
All right.

02:43.400 --> 02:46.040
So now let's go and query those informations.

02:46.080 --> 02:48.680
Now we have to analyze the yearly performance.

02:48.680 --> 02:50.680
That means we don't need the day.

02:50.720 --> 02:52.640
The granularity is the years.

02:52.640 --> 02:55.880
So that's why let's go and convert it using year function.

02:55.880 --> 02:58.320
And we're going to call it order year.

02:58.360 --> 03:01.480
And of course we have to go and aggregate then the sales.

03:01.480 --> 03:04.320
And I'm going to call it current sales.

03:04.480 --> 03:11.760
And of course we have to group up the data datum by the date, the year, and as well by the product

03:11.800 --> 03:12.240
name.

03:12.840 --> 03:13.800
So that's it.

03:13.800 --> 03:15.240
Let's go and execute it.

03:15.240 --> 03:18.240
And of course I'm going to go and get rid of all those nulls.

03:18.440 --> 03:22.440
So where order date is not null.

03:22.480 --> 03:22.960
All right.

03:22.960 --> 03:25.440
So with that we have solved the first part.

03:25.480 --> 03:28.160
So we have the yearly performance of the products.

03:28.200 --> 03:34.200
Now in the task we have to compare this value that current sales to the average sales performance of

03:34.200 --> 03:34.880
the products.

03:34.880 --> 03:39.320
So that means we need the average and as well the previous year's sales.

03:39.320 --> 03:43.960
So that means we have to compare each value to the previous year for the same product of course.

03:43.960 --> 03:47.200
So that means things are getting a little bit more complicated.

03:47.200 --> 03:49.840
And with that we need the help of the window functions.

03:49.840 --> 03:51.040
Let's do it one by one.

03:51.040 --> 03:52.800
Let's focus on the average sales.

03:52.800 --> 03:55.280
So now what we're going to do based on those values.

03:55.280 --> 03:59.760
Based on these results we will do a new calculations and aggregations.

03:59.800 --> 04:03.880
And now in order to do that either we use a subquery or a CTE.

04:03.920 --> 04:06.400
I'm going to go with the CTE because it looks nicer.

04:06.400 --> 04:09.920
So with yearly product sales.

04:09.960 --> 04:13.320
This is the new name that you are giving for these results.

04:13.320 --> 04:16.880
And now what we're going to do, we're going to build queries on top of these results.

04:16.880 --> 04:20.560
So first of all I will just select everything from this table.

04:20.600 --> 04:22.720
Yearly product sales just to test.

04:22.720 --> 04:23.760
So it is working.

04:23.760 --> 04:26.680
Now I'm selecting data from our CTE.

04:26.720 --> 04:31.000
So now the next step I'm going to go and list all the columns that I want in my results.

04:31.000 --> 04:36.360
So the order date, the product name the current sales.

04:36.960 --> 04:37.920
This is just nicer.

04:37.920 --> 04:42.040
In order to have control on which columns you want to present at the end results.

04:42.160 --> 04:49.120
Now the next step, I'm going to go and order the data by first the product name and then the order

04:49.160 --> 04:49.600
here.

04:49.600 --> 04:52.280
And with that we can have better understanding of the results.

04:52.280 --> 04:55.320
So we can see this product has three years of sales.

04:55.360 --> 04:57.880
And those are the current sales for each year.

04:57.880 --> 05:02.240
So now we have to go and calculate the average of those three sales.

05:02.240 --> 05:08.520
So in order to do that we're going to use the average current sales over.

05:09.160 --> 05:11.600
We have to decide now how to partition the data.

05:11.640 --> 05:16.440
Since we are focusing on the products, we have to partition the results by the product name.

05:16.440 --> 05:20.120
So we're going to say partition by product name.

05:20.520 --> 05:23.480
And we don't have to sort the data because we are using the average.

05:23.480 --> 05:25.840
So it doesn't matter how the data is sorted.

05:25.840 --> 05:28.120
So let's call it average sales.

05:28.120 --> 05:29.760
So let's go and execute it.

05:29.800 --> 05:35.320
And now if you are looking to the results for this product, the average sales of all the three values

05:35.360 --> 05:36.880
is 13,000.

05:36.920 --> 05:43.400
So now as you can see for each row we have the current sales and side by side with the average sales

05:43.400 --> 05:45.920
and the same thing for the next product as well.

05:45.920 --> 05:51.200
So now since we have both of the informations on the same row, current sales and the average, the

05:51.200 --> 05:55.240
change, the difference between the current value and the average value.

05:55.240 --> 05:57.320
So all what you have to do is to go and subtract.

05:57.320 --> 05:57.720
Right.

05:57.720 --> 06:03.360
So we're going to say the current sales subtracted by the average sales.

06:03.360 --> 06:07.520
And we're going to call it the difference in average.

06:07.520 --> 06:09.480
So let's go and execute it.

06:09.480 --> 06:15.370
And now as you can see we are getting now The comparison we have, the differences between the current

06:15.370 --> 06:16.210
and the average.

06:16.210 --> 06:21.770
And of course, what I like to do is to make a flag or like indicator, whether we are above the average

06:21.770 --> 06:23.530
or below the average or at the average.

06:23.530 --> 06:26.890
So in order to do that, we're going to go and use the case statement.

06:26.890 --> 06:35.610
So if the difference is higher than zero then we are above the average right above average.

06:35.650 --> 06:37.530
Or let's have an abbreviation for that.

06:37.570 --> 06:41.890
And if we are below zero that means we are below the average right.

06:41.890 --> 06:45.450
So below then below average.

06:45.650 --> 06:49.650
And if it is exactly zero else then it is average.

06:49.650 --> 06:50.970
So that's it.

06:51.010 --> 06:51.890
Let's end it.

06:52.090 --> 06:54.450
And I'm going to call it average change.

06:55.130 --> 06:57.130
So let's go and execute it.

06:57.130 --> 07:03.610
Now if you focus again on one of the products you can see the current sales of this product in 2012.

07:03.650 --> 07:05.210
It is below the average.

07:05.210 --> 07:06.050
It is really low.

07:06.050 --> 07:10.650
And for the next year for 2013 it is above the average.

07:10.690 --> 07:16.770
It was really nice year for this product and the last year 2014, it was again below the average.

07:16.770 --> 07:22.450
So with that we have really nice flag in order to see quickly whether we are above or below the average.

07:22.450 --> 07:25.410
And it is interesting to see whether we have zeros.

07:25.410 --> 07:28.370
So yeah, sometimes it is exactly like the average.

07:28.370 --> 07:29.770
And here we have like zero.

07:29.810 --> 07:31.370
It's not below or above.

07:31.410 --> 07:37.330
So with that we are comparing the performance of the sales of each products with the average.

07:37.330 --> 07:40.890
And as you can see it is really simple using the window functions.

07:40.890 --> 07:42.690
So let's go and check again our task.

07:42.690 --> 07:46.130
We have compared the current sales to the average sales performance.

07:46.170 --> 07:50.330
Now we have to compare it as well with the previous year's sales.

07:50.330 --> 07:52.850
So let's go back to our example over here.

07:52.850 --> 07:58.250
This time we have to compare the current sales not with the average but with the previous year.

07:58.250 --> 08:01.730
So we don't have to write like another CTE or query.

08:01.770 --> 08:03.890
We can continue with the same results.

08:03.890 --> 08:07.250
So now all what we have to do is to access the previous year.

08:07.250 --> 08:10.770
And in order to do that we have amazing window function called lag.

08:10.770 --> 08:12.250
So let's do it step by step.

08:12.250 --> 08:15.930
So now we're going to go and create a new column called Old lag.

08:15.970 --> 08:19.090
I want to access the previous value of what?

08:19.090 --> 08:20.010
The current sales.

08:20.010 --> 08:20.330
Right.

08:20.330 --> 08:23.210
So current sales and over.

08:24.130 --> 08:28.410
We still have to partition the data by the product name because we focus on the products.

08:28.410 --> 08:31.530
So partition by product name.

08:31.890 --> 08:36.770
But now in order to access the previous value, that means we have to sort the data and we're going

08:36.770 --> 08:40.010
to sort it by the years we need the previous year.

08:40.010 --> 08:45.410
So we're going to say order by order year, and we're going to sort it ascending from the lowest to

08:45.450 --> 08:46.050
the highest.

08:46.050 --> 08:47.610
So we're going to leave it like this.

08:47.610 --> 08:52.690
And with that, this window function is going to give us the previous years sales of the products.

08:52.690 --> 08:56.170
So I'm just going to call it previous year sales like this.

08:56.210 --> 08:58.290
And I think here we have something wrong okay.

08:58.330 --> 09:02.330
So let's go and execute it and let's go and focus on one of those products.

09:02.330 --> 09:06.490
So now for the first year of this product the previous year was null right.

09:06.490 --> 09:09.290
So we don't have any data from the previous year.

09:09.290 --> 09:13.170
But for the 2013 we have a previous year of 2012.

09:13.170 --> 09:19.010
So that's why now we are getting the previous value of the sales based on the years and the same thing

09:19.010 --> 09:20.010
for the last year.

09:20.050 --> 09:23.210
Over here you can see we are getting the previous sales.

09:23.210 --> 09:24.370
So it is working.

09:24.370 --> 09:26.410
And for the next window, same thing.

09:26.410 --> 09:31.130
For the first year you will get null and the previous sales will get it from the previous year.

09:31.130 --> 09:33.690
So with that we have now the previous sales.

09:33.690 --> 09:39.490
And if you check this over here, we have in the same row now the current sales of the current year

09:39.490 --> 09:42.770
and as well the sales of the previous year.

09:42.850 --> 09:44.610
Now what we have to do the same thing.

09:44.610 --> 09:48.610
We have to go and subtract those two informations in order to compare them.

09:48.610 --> 09:49.010
Right.

09:49.050 --> 09:51.010
So we're going to go and do the same thing.

09:51.130 --> 09:56.530
So we will get the current sales minus the whole thing, the whole window function.

09:56.530 --> 09:59.290
And we can call it previous year.

09:59.410 --> 10:01.290
So difference of the previous year.

10:01.290 --> 10:04.330
And with that we are calculating the differences between them.

10:04.330 --> 10:09.570
So for this year for this product as you can see the difference here is really big between the current

10:09.570 --> 10:10.970
sales and the previous year.

10:11.010 --> 10:15.250
Now of course what we can do, we can go and make as well a flag or an indicator.

10:15.250 --> 10:18.890
I'm going to go and copy the whole thing from the previous average.

10:18.890 --> 10:22.130
But we have to go and get the right function.

10:22.250 --> 10:23.890
This are the same over here.

10:23.930 --> 10:26.210
And now it is not above or below the average.

10:26.210 --> 10:29.490
I'm going to say it is increasing or decreasing right.

10:29.530 --> 10:31.970
So increase or decrease.

10:32.490 --> 10:34.770
And we're going to call it previous year change.

10:34.810 --> 10:37.210
And instead of average we can say no change.

10:37.730 --> 10:40.090
So let's go and execute it.

10:40.090 --> 10:42.090
And I'm having here an extra comma.

10:42.130 --> 10:43.570
Let's go and execute it.

10:43.570 --> 10:46.650
So again let's go and focus of one of those products.

10:46.650 --> 10:51.050
For the first year of this product there is no change because there is no previous year.

10:51.050 --> 10:55.090
For the next year of this product we have an increase, right.

10:55.130 --> 10:58.650
Because the current sales is way higher than the previous year.

10:58.690 --> 11:03.970
And now by going to the last year of this product, we have a decrease because the current sales is

11:03.970 --> 11:05.810
less than the previous year.

11:05.810 --> 11:11.810
So my friends, we call this type of analysis year over year analysis.

11:11.810 --> 11:15.650
And if you want to calculate the month over month analysis, it's very simple.

11:15.810 --> 11:19.690
All you have to do is to go and change the function from year to a month.

11:19.730 --> 11:25.170
And with that you are extracting the month part, and the difference between analyzing the months and

11:25.170 --> 11:27.250
years is of course, the scope.

11:27.290 --> 11:32.570
Year over year is good for long term trends analyses where on the other hand, the month over month

11:32.570 --> 11:35.290
it is short term trends analysis.

11:35.290 --> 11:38.370
You are just focusing on the seasonality of your data.

11:38.370 --> 11:44.610
So this is how we analyze the performance of our business by comparing the current measure with a target

11:44.610 --> 11:45.050
measure.

11:45.050 --> 11:47.970
And you can go and use different dimensions and stuff.

11:47.970 --> 11:53.170
So instead of the sales you can check the quantity instead of products, you can check the customers

11:53.170 --> 11:58.250
and you can go and compare the current information not only with the average or the previous year.

11:58.250 --> 12:04.210
You can compare it with the lowest sales and the highest sales, and it can open the door for many different

12:04.210 --> 12:04.850
insights.

12:04.850 --> 12:07.330
But we are always using the same methods.

12:07.330 --> 12:13.450
Using the window functions, we compare the current value with another value in our datasets.

12:13.490 --> 12:15.650
So this is how we do performance comparison.

12:15.690 --> 12:16.010
All right.

12:16.010 --> 12:20.050
So with that you have learned how to analyze the performance of our business.

12:20.050 --> 12:23.290
